AD  - Department of Information Technology, Faculty of Science and Engineering, Al-Hikma University, Sana'a, Yemen
AB  - Telehealth and mHealth apps are expanding rapidly, yet user discontinuation remains high (>45%), with many users leaving soon after adoption. This research develops an integrated analytical framework that synthesizes supervised machine learning (ML) classifiers with Machine-Assisted Thematic Analysis (MATA) to provide both predictive accuracy and interpretative depth regarding user attrition. A total of 9,684 reviews from Google Play and Apple iOS stores in the Middle East were collected, of which 6,628 were used to train five classifiers. While KNN and SGD classifiers effectively predict continued usage, thematic findings indicate that security concerns and privacy risks serve as primary catalysts for user abandonment. To address these specific retention barriers, the study develops a secure blockchain-based framework designed to restore user trust through decentralized data integrity and auditable access control. The proposed framework delivers both accurate prediction and actionable explanations to support real-time monitoring and targeted improvements in telehealth app engagement and retention. In addition, the study proposes a secure blockchain-based telehealth framework to mitigate the identified security and privacy issues. The proposed scheme is designed to satisfy key telehealth security requirements, including confidentiality, secure data access control, auditability, and traceability. The transition from predictive modelling to architectural design was necessitated by the qualitative evidence that technical security is a fundamental prerequisite for sustained user retention. This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
