AD  - Institute for Energy Technology, Halden, Norway
AB  - This research explores the feasibility of using handheld Augmented Reality (AR) technology for on-site human factors control room design review and evaluation. Building on previous studies demonstrating the effectiveness of iterative, user-centered design processes supported by 3D tools, it investigates AR's potential to facilitate evaluations aligned with industrial standards and to support on-site prototyping. The methodology is a use-case driven evaluation using an Apple iPad Pro software prototype that integrates mobile AR with well-known evaluation methods from 3D control room design software. While 3D technologies and CAD tools are commonly used in the nuclear industry to support control room design, they require detailed 3D models upfront, which can be costly to produce, especially in stepwise or multi-stage modernization approaches. Findings indicate that AR has significant potential for on-site evaluation of control room ergonomics, reducing the need for complex 3D model production and accelerating the assessment speed. AR's effectiveness stems from its ability to utilize existing physical surroundings, making it advantageous for control room modernization projects. Handheld devices are also suitable for 3D scanning of control rooms, capable of capturing large surfaces quickly and accurately. © 2025 AMERICAN NUCLEAR SOCIETY, INCORPORATED, WESTMONT, ILLINOIS 60559.
