AD  - Stanford Doerr School of Sustainability, Stanford Univ, United States
AD  - Dept. of Civil and Environmental Engineering, Villanova Univ, United States
AD  - Dept. of Computer Science, Stanford Univ, United States
AD  - Byers Center for Biodesign, Stanford Univ, United States
AB  - Indoor environmental features, especially natural elements, are increasingly recognized as crucial for enhancing human wellbeing. Our study, adopting a long-Term, naturalistic approach, advances this understanding through a Hybrid Physical + Digital Spaces (HPDS) platform. We conducted a 4-week pilot study (N = 7), with participants working in experimentally controlled office environments. Each week, participants were assigned an experimental condition based on two controlled variables: windows (with or without) and furniture material (natural or artificial). Participants wore a commercial smartwatch and responded to daily questionnaires distributed through a custom-made iOS app that also logged health metrics and office presence. Compliance rules included spending at least 3 h in the office for four weekdays each week, wearing the smartwatch for over 18 h daily, and responding to at least half of the daily and weekly surveys. This approach allowed for comprehensive data collection on biometric readings, indoor environmental characteristics, and subjective wellbeing using a mixed-methods approach. Our findings from multiple performance assessments and participants' feedback provide valuable lessons for future naturalistic field study designs using an integrated platform to synthesize measured outcomes. The scalability and adaptability of such methods suggest promising potential for researchers and practitioners in the fields of building design and computer science. © 2024 ASCE.
